<div class="legal-layout"><nav id="legal-nav" class="legal-nav" aria-label="On this page"></nav><article class="legal-copy">
<p class="lead">HerMap is a free public-interest initiative. We collect limited structured report information to review community concerns and publish broad patterns.</p>
<h2>What a report saves</h2><p>A submitted report saves its experience category, place type, selected state/city/post office and PIN, incident date and approximate time in IST, a broad 0.25° map cell, review status and submission time. Optional public venue name and website, and an optional online account username and platform, are encrypted together for authorised private review. Account details are unverified context, not proof of wrongdoing; do not include your own handle, real names, contact details or a child’s account. We do not request personal names, email, phone numbers, identity documents, photos or private addresses.</p><p>You may also choose a point on the local map, enter latitude/longitude, or explicitly request device location. Exact coordinates are submitted and encrypted only if you check the separate private-location sharing option. They assign a broad public cell and are visible only to authorised moderators. A precise point can identify a sensitive place; avoid homes or survivor/child identifiers. You can report without sharing it. Only submit information you are comfortable sharing for these purposes. The form requires acknowledgement of this notice and the terms, and confirmation that you are 18 or older. You can withdraw a report using its private receipt. Not needing a public account does not guarantee anonymity from every hosting system or lawful authority.</p>
<h2>What becomes public</h2><p>Individual records, account usernames/platforms, venue names, exact shared coordinates, precise incident times and receipts are never shown on the public map. Approved reports wait at least <?= PUBLICATION_DELAY_HOURS ?> hours after review. Groups below <?= PUBLIC_MIN_REPORTS ?> reports are withheld; eligible counts are shown in bands of five. Patterns use broad map cells and report windows of 30, 90 or 365 days. The site-wide received-report counter includes submissions before review and retains historic submission totals after deletion.</p>
<h2>Necessary cookies and security</h2><p>A first-party session cookie, HERMAP_SESSION, protects the human check, form requests and moderator login. It is HttpOnly, SameSite Strict and secure on HTTPS. It is not an advertising cookie. Session state contains a random form token, a temporary maths answer and, for authorised staff, login state. Application rate limits use a keyed, rotating hash derived from the network address and time window; raw IP addresses are not stored in incident records or rate-limit tables. This is an abuse-prevention measure, not a visitor profile.</p><p>Form validation, a single-use maths check, honeypot, duplicate checks and limits reduce automated misuse. They do not verify identity or prove that an incident happened. Moderator access requires a password and an authenticator code. Public deployment requires HTTPS.</p>
<h2>Visits and live counters</h2><p>Each valid public-page load increases the daily visit total. Admin and API requests do not add visits. This counts page visits, including reloads and separate tabs, not unique people or guaranteed human visitors. A random token kept only in the current page updates its active status while visible. The database stores only its hash and expiry. “Active visits” means visits with activity in the last two minutes. We do not use advertising, fingerprinting, visitor accounts, browsing profiles or third-party behavioural analytics.</p>
<h2>Search, maps and location</h2><p>PIN/post-office search queries and requests for grouped area statistics go to HerMap. Map outlines, scripts, fonts and icons are served by HerMap; the map does not request third-party political raster tiles. Search data is a historical PIN directory enriched with approximate coordinates, and can contain errors.</p><p>Location permission is optional. Exact device coordinates stay in the current page. Only the broad grid cell is sent to request an aggregate summary. This main-map lookup is not saved as visitor history or attached to a report. The reporting form’s separate optional location picker sends chosen coordinates only when you explicitly opt in and submit; these are encrypted with the report as explained above. Browser/device location services operate under their own practices. Request paths, including search queries or broad grid cells, may appear in infrastructure logs.</p>
<h2>Retention, deletion and access</h2><p>Incident records and encrypted venue/online/location context have a 400-day expiry from submission. Expired records are removed by bounded cleanup batches on counter requests or by the maintenance command; an idle installation can retain expired records until cleanup runs. A valid receipt removes the report from the active database immediately. Only a receipt hash is stored. Keep your receipt private: anyone who has it can check or delete your report, and lost receipts cannot be recovered.</p><p>Expired live-visit tokens remain for up to one day after last expiry before cleanup. Expired rate-limit entries are removed on cleanup. Non-identifying daily visit/report counters remain for historical graphs; moderation actions expire after 400 days and may remain after a report is deleted. Necessary session files are managed by PHP session expiry/garbage collection. These are application rules, not a promise about infrastructure logs, email, backups or lawful preservation.</p><p>Infrastructure logging, backup retention, processor agreements, incident response and lawful preservation procedures must be confirmed before public deployment. Where applicable, statutory security/logging obligations, including CERT-In directions, take precedence. Legal requests must be assessed by the responsible operator. The development installation is not a certification of production compliance.</p>
<h2>Support and your choices</h2><p>For questions, correction, privacy, deletion or misuse concerns, email <a href="mailto:<?= ps_escape(SUPPORT_EMAIL) ?>"><?= ps_escape(SUPPORT_EMAIL) ?></a>. We receive your address and message through the email provider; please send only what is necessary and avoid sensitive narratives or identity documents. Receipt controls are available without email. Designated grievance details and applicable response/escalation procedures remain requirements before public deployment.</p>
<h2>Sharing and children</h2><p>We do not sell report information. Access is restricted to authorised moderators and necessary service providers. Valid legal obligations can require preservation, disclosure or removal, and absolute confidentiality cannot be promised. Reporting is initially for adults aged 18 and over; do not identify children or survivors. Child-protection and mandatory-reporting duties require a separate operational process and are not removed by an age checkbox. Emergency and support links remain accessible to everyone.</p>
<h2>India and legal review</h2><p>The initial service is for India. Operator identity, hosting location, service providers and any transfers must be documented before public deployment. Future countries require their own review. Review must address applicable Indian law, including the Information Technology Act and rules, child-protection and security obligations, and the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and Rules, 2025 as provisions commence. The rules have phased commencement; this draft does not claim every provision is currently in force or guarantee legal immunity.</p>
<div class="policy-sources"><h2>Official references</h2><p><a href="https://www.meity.gov.in/static/uploads/2025/11/53450e6e5dc0bfa85ebd78686cadad39.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MeitY: Digital Personal Data Protection Rules, 2025</a> · <a href="https://www.cert-in.org.in/Directions70B.jsp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CERT-In directions and FAQs</a>. These guide review; they do not certify HerMap’s compliance.</p></div>
